transitioning from full-time distance  education that's the consideration for  clark county school district board of  trustees that's going to happen this  thursday  now not a lot of details are known about  what that may look like but  8 news now reporter kristen drummond  received some insight about the possible  plan  the district has not yet publicly posted  about a possible transition plan but  is trying to gather feedback and needs  parents to answer a questionnaire  tonight  we have two options giving families a  choice  i would really like for them to be back  in school at least even if it's just a  few days  paula guaman answers a ccsd survey with  four  questions it asks parents to decide  which educational option works best for  their students  going forward it was either online  learning for the rest of the year  or having the hybrid which is two days  in person and three days online  the other three questions deal with  transportation logistics  it's just something that's on the air  uncertainty as  ccsd develops a transition plan from  what i understand at this point  it would not be an entire day principal  tam larnard at spring valley high school  tells me  what parents can likely expect at the  high school level  from what i'm understanding is that  physically the students would be here in  the morning  and then the remainder of the day they  would be at home  attending school virtually in the  afternoon after after lunch  lernard says that allows the custodial  staff to clean the entire afternoon  get ready for the next students that  come the next day a safety consideration  as  families choose the best option for  their child kristin drummond 8 news now  and we can tell you just moments ago  ccsd announced that it's now going to  extend the survey deadline because they  do want to hear from more families  but they didn't say until when a date  has not been set for implementation of  that hybrid model if trustees  do indeed approve it on thursday
 

